"hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:"
3063,"**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us Ibn Wahb, that Malik informed them, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered Makkah in the Year of the Conquest, and on his head was the *mighfar* (a kind of helmet). When he removed it, a man came to him and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, Ibn Abi Khatal is clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah.""

So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, ""Kill him.""

Ibn Shihab said, ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was not in a state of *ihram* (pilgrim sanctity) that day.""

---"
3064,"Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Isa, who narrated to us Salama, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ja'far ibn al-Fadl ibn al-Hasan ibn Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent me, and sent with me a man from the Ansar, and he (ﷺ) said: ""Go to Abu Sufyan ibn Harb and kill him."" Then he recounted the rest of the incident, saying: When we entered Mecca, my companion said to me: ""Would you like us to first circumambulate the Sacred House (Kaaba) seven times, then pray two rak'ahs?"" I replied: ""I know the people of Mecca better than anyone: when darkness falls, they close their homes and sit inside them, and I am aware of a black horse among them."" He continued to urge me until we reached the House, so we circumambulated it seven times and prayed two rak'ahs, then we departed."
